A media scan is slow because every TV episode is probed with `ffprobe` on every scan, and each file then triggers a chain of SQLite statements that each commit on their own.

## What dominates: `ffprobe` on every episode

`scan_content_directories` walks each configured folder and, for every `*.mp4`, builds a row and inserts it:

```183:188:app/database/db_getter.py
    def scan_content_directories(self):
        if content_directory_list := self.get_all_content_directory_info():
            print(f"Scanning: {content_directory_list}")
            for content_directory_info in content_directory_list:
                self.collect_directory_content(content_directory_info)
        print("Scan Complete")
```

Movies and books only parse the filename. TV does not. `build_tv_show` calls `get_ffmpeg_metadata` for every episode:

```162:163:app/database/media_metadata_collector.py
    if mp4_file_path.exists():
        get_ffmpeg_metadata(mp4_file_path, content_data)
```

That runs `ffmpeg.probe`, which starts a new `ffprobe` process and reads the file’s container header:

```71:79:app/database/media_metadata_collector.py
def get_ffmpeg_metadata(path, content_data):
    try:
        if ffmpeg_probe_result := ffmpeg.probe(path):
            if ffmpeg_probe_result_format := ffmpeg_probe_result.get('format'):
                if tags := ffmpeg_probe_result_format.get('tags'):
                    content_data["content_title"] = tags.get('title', '')
```

The only value kept is the title tag. Duration is commented out. If the tag is missing, the title falls back to `Episode {n}` from the filename. There is no mtime or size check, so a rescan probes files that are already in the catalog.

`ffprobe` is sequential. A few hundred milliseconds per file is normal, and it is worse when the `moov` atom sits at the end of a large MP4 or the files are on a spinning disk or a network mount. A few thousand episodes is many minutes of process startup and file seeks, before the database work.

## What adds more time: one commit per statement

Each insert goes through `add_data_to_db` → `execute_db_query`, which commits immediately:

```60:64:app/database/db_access.py
    def execute_db_query(self, query, cursor, params=()):
        if sqlite3.complete_statement(query):
            try:
                cursor.execute(query, params)
                self.connection.commit()
```

One TV file is a show container, a season container, and an episode. For each of those, a repeat scan still does `INSERT OR IGNORE`, and when the row already exists it follows with a `SELECT` for the id. Show and season tag links run again on every episode of that show (`insert_container` always rewrites tags, even when the container insert was ignored). With SQLite’s default full synchronous mode, each of those commits is its own fsync.

`collect_mp4_files` also does `list(path.rglob("*.mp4"))`, so the whole tree is listed before the first insert. That walk is cheap next to `ffprobe`.

## When it gets worse

In CLIENT mode, `start_scan_media_directories(run_client_sync=True)` scans, may pull files from the server, then scans the library a second time if the sync asks for a rescan. That doubles the probe-and-commit work.

